A||N||4||chris6878||Lug Nuts are backing off..  why?||||5||
Z||000000||chris6878||03-21-2000||12:54 AM||||What would cause your lug nuts to back off of you rims...   First i noticed my back drivers side tire making a slight noise on the road while driving... i inspected it and my lugs where a little loose..     i tighten them and go on..       a few days later.. same scenario, except on of my studs is gone where u put one of the lugs on..    what the  **** is going on back there...???||||reg||5
Z||000001||Luperman||03-21-2000||01:09 AM||||did u torque them down or maybe somebody is loosening them up while u sleep?||||reg||1
Z||000002||chris6878||03-21-2000||01:20 AM||||i was thinking the same.. but i dont think thats my answer..  lol...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000003||batboy||03-21-2000||07:26 AM||||The wheel stud broke because the wheel was loose and broke it. The other lug bolts may be weakened too. I'd replace them all on that corner. Are you torquing the wheels down to specs? (100 ft/lbs for alloy wheels) If so, I'm at a loss. Are you sure nobody's mad at you? Maybe the lug bolts have been over tightened in the past and now they're stressed and stretching, dunno.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||03-21-2000||01:55 PM||||I have seen rims ruined by driving with loose nuts. Especially true with alum/mag wheels. The loose nuts will "wallow out" the rim to the point where they will not really tightening against the rim. Look at the rim closely and compare to another. You might want to take it to a trusted shop to make sure the rim is OK. Having a wheel come of is a definite safety problem!!!<BR>||||reg||1
